        "timestamp": 1782448631
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you do the following for both of the inductives, what does it report?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inductInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">info</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">getConstInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">``sub_is_def_v3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">throwError</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">logInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m!</span><span class=\"s2\">\"params: {info.numParams}, indices: {info.numIndices}\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 606656341,
        "sender_full_name": "Max Nowak 🐉",
        "timestamp": 1782459609
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inductInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">info</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">getConstInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">``sub_is_def_v3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">throwError</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">logInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m!</span><span class=\"s2\">\"params: {info.numParams}, indices: {info.numIndices}\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>params: 0, indices: 3</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inductInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">info</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">getConstInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">``sub_is_def_v3_alt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">throwError</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">logInfo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m!</span><span class=\"s2\">\"params: {info.numParams}, indices: {info.numIndices}\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>params: 2, indices: 1</p>",
        "id": 606770871,
        "sender_full_name": "Paige Thomas",
        "timestamp": 1782493338
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For inductive types, parameters and type indices are treated differently. A parameter is uniform, it must be the same in all constructors -- an example is the <code>α</code> in <code>List : (α : Type) -&gt; Type</code>. Type indices may vary -- an example is the <code>k</code> in <code>Vec : (α : Type) -&gt; (k : Nat) -&gt; Type</code>. I've omitted the ctors because I assume you know those types. Note that <code>Vec.nil : {α : Type} -&gt; Vec α 0</code> has no extra binder for <code>k</code>, but <code>Vec.cons</code> does have an extra binder for <code>k</code>. Similarly in the methods (i.e. the cases for nil, cons) on the eliminator <code>Vec.rec</code>, they need to provide these binders, mirroring the ctors. Contrast that with the parameters, e.g. the <code>α</code> in <code>List.rec</code>, which can be pulled \"out\" / \"in front of\" of the cases, because it's uniform among all the cases anyway.</p>",
        "id": 607088978,
        "sender_full_name": "Max Nowak 🐉",
        "timestamp": 1782721421
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you inspect <code>sub_is_def_v3.rec</code> and <code>sub_is_def_v3_alt.rec</code> you'll see exactly this. <code>sub_is_def_v3_alt</code> will have more params pulled out in front. In general, <code>inductive Foo (Params...) : (Indices...) -&gt; Sort _</code>, but Lean sometimes tries to be smart and promote the indices to parameters.</p>",
        "id": 607089443,
        "sender_full_name": "Max Nowak 🐉",
        "timestamp": 1782721575
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry for the very late reply. Thank you!</p>",
